## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design advanced observability with safe overhead, tenant isolation, and rollback.

## Key Concepts
- eBPF tracepoints, container lifecycle, per-tenant tagging, tail-latency analysis, rollout and rollback.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-eBPF sketch: tag events with tenant and record startup latency
tracepoint("container_start", (ctx) => {
  const tenant = getCurrentTenantId();
  recordLatency(tenant, now() - startTime);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate that tracing does not perturb startup times under load?
- What metrics and dashboards would you expose to operators?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical mastery of Linux memory isolation, NUMA binding, and low-latency multitenant workloads under fragmentation pressure.

## Key Concepts
- memcg v2 quotas, cpuset, NUMA bindings
- HugePages/THP strategy; allocator isolation (jemalloc arenas)
- kernel memory.compaction tuning; vmscan knobs
- per-tenant BPF observability; staged rollouts

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal eBPF: count major page allocations per cgroup id
#include <uapi/linux/ptrace.h>
BPF_HASH(page_allocs, u64, u64);
int trace_alloc(struct trace_event_raw_alloc *e) {
  u64 cid = bpf_get_current_uid_gid();
  page_allocs.increment(cid);
  return 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify fragmentation impact and rollback criteria?
- Which tenants get priority during compaction?

## Why This Is Asked
Examines ability to tame CPU scheduling and memory reclaim in multi-tenant Linux deployments under bursty workloads.

## Key Concepts
- cgroup v2 cpu.max and CPU weight for per-tenant QoS
- cpuset and CPU affinity to confine tenants
- memory reclaim behavior and swappiness tuning
- BPF-based observability for per-tenant latency, IRQs, cache misses
- phased testing, safe rollback, and controlled rollouts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Lightweight latency probe template (pseudo)
async function measureLatency(endpoint){
  const t0 = performance.now();
  await fetch(endpoint);
  return performance.now() - t0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify safe rollback criteria during staged tests?
- Which metrics dashboards and alerts would you configure for sustained SLOs?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical diagnostic discipline and concrete mitigations for a real-world, multi-tenant file-watching workload. It emphasizes measurable tracing, safe kernel tuning, and robust rollback procedures.

## Key Concepts

- Inotify vs fanotify event storms
- Per-tenant isolation with cgroup2
- Kernel tunables fs.inotify.* and event quotas
- eBPF-based latency measurement and filtering
- Staged load testing and rollback strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple token bucket limiter for event bursts (illustrative)
let tokens = 1000;
function allow(n) {
  if (tokens >= n) { tokens -= n; return true; }
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement per-tenant quota policy changes in a Kubernetes-based deployment?
- What exact metrics would you monitor to confirm the problem is resolved and no data loss occurred?
